<h2 id="why">Why It Hasn't Mattered</h2>
<p>At 6-foot-4 with a 61.1% career contested-catch rate, Boston's game was never built to be won with a stopwatch. His improved vertical jump between the combine and pro day — an unusual and deliberate uptick that scouts specifically flagged — signaled real explosiveness in exactly the area that matters for a big-bodied X-receiver: winning 50-50 balls above defenders rather than separating from them in the open field. Scouts compared his profile to Michael Pittman Jr., another size-and-catch-radius receiver who was never considered a burner but has built a productive NFL career anyway.</p>
<p>Two games into his career, that scouting report is playing out almost exactly as written. Boston's touchdowns have come on contested, high-point catches rather than blown coverage — precisely the outcome his vertical jump and catch-radius profile predicted, and precisely the reason a missing 40 time never worried the team that drafted him.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Speed Isn't the Only Number That Matters</strong>
  A receiver's 40 time gets the most attention, but for a contested-catch specialist like Boston, the vertical jump and body control are the numbers that actually predict production. He skipped the marquee test twice and it hasn't cost him a single target.
</div>
